---
/**
 * The /workshops FAQ (BLD-183, layout WL-1b, WL-2a: after the tickets, before the pilot). Final copy 1g,
 * "Why this format works" (1d) as "Why this format?" (the pilot result lives in the Pilot section only), "What comes after W6?" (CR-4) and the gap answers Q5, Q7, Q9, Q14, Q16,
 * Q20, Q22 and Q29 from the rollout map §3. FAQ review 17 Nov (FQ-1 to FQ-24, FQ-28): booking questions first,
 * new answers on minimum size, research, access, after the day and the certificate. Access rule 17 Nov (terms v2.0 §4):
 * lessons and AI tutor until 30 days after each workshop; the daily question cap is stated in the terms only. FAQ dedup
 * 17 Nov (workshop-planning/review/faq-dedup-2026-11-17.md): each fact once; "miss one on the pass" folded into
 * refunds and "after the day". Three groups, each with a riso chip; every question has a
 * numbered square in its group colour, as the lesson numbers on the cards. Long answers are bullets.
 * Rendered only with PUBLIC_WORKSHOP_SERIES=true (it lives inside brand/Series.astro).
 */
import { TICKETS } from '@/data/workshops/tickets';
import { SERIES } from '@/data/workshops/series';
import { bandKey } from '@/components/workshops/seriesFormat';

interface Item { q: string; id?: string; open?: boolean; lines?: string[]; points?: string[]; html?: string; next?: boolean }
interface Group { name: string; fill: string; items: Item[] }

const cite = (n: number) => `<sup><a href="#fmt-ref-${n}" aria-label="Reference ${n}">${n}</a></sup>`;

const GROUPS: Group[] = [
  {
    name: 'Before you book',
    fill: 'fill-c2',
    items: [
      {
        q: 'Do I need to know Python?',
        points: [
          'Not for W1. It starts from zero, in small steps.',
          'Never written code? Do the warm-up that comes with your code first: about an hour, in two parts (Python, then NumPy).',
          'From W2, beginner Python helps (variables, loops, functions).',
        ],
      },
      {
        q: 'Will my laptop work?',
        points: [
          'Any Windows, Mac or Linux laptop with a current Chrome or Firefox.',
          "Tablets and phones don't work for the lessons.",
          'The 2-minute laptop check in your setup email tells you for sure.',
        ],
      },
      {
        q: 'Can I come to just one?',
        lines: [
          'Yes. Each workshop stands on its own and starts with a 15-minute recap.',
          'Joining after W1 with a single ticket? You also get a short recap page of the earlier workshops.',
          'Single tickets for W2 to W6 open later; until then the pass is the way to book them.',
        ],
      },
      {
        q: 'Can I get a refund?',
        html:
          '<ul class="fmt-points">' +
          '<li>Single ticket: full refund up to 7 days before the workshop.</li>' +
          '<li>Series pass: up to 7 days before your first workshop.</li>' +
          '<li>After that, send someone in your place (tell us by 12:00 the day before) or move to another workshop with a free seat. With the pass, you can send someone to any workshop you miss.</li>' +
          `<li>Details in the <a href="${TICKETS.terms}">ticket terms</a>.</li>` +
          '</ul>',
      },
      {
        q: 'What if too few people book?',
        lines: [
          'If fewer than 4 people have booked 7 days before, we move or cancel the workshop.',
          'You choose: keep your seat for the new date or get a full refund.',
        ],
      },
      { q: 'Can my teenager come?', lines: ['Everyone must be 18 or older.'] },
      {
        q: 'Do you have access needs?',
        html: `<p>Write to <a href="mailto:workshops@pixels2genai.art">workshops@pixels2genai.art</a> before you book and we'll tell you about the studio.</p>`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Is this research? Do I have to take part?',
        lines: [
          'The series is part of research on teaching creative coding.',
          "Taking part is optional: you're asked separately when you enter your code.",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'The lessons',
    fill: 'fill-c1',
    items: [
      {
        q: 'Why this format?',
        id: 'faq-why',
        html:
          '<ul class="fmt-points">' +
          `<li><b>You learn by doing.</b> Across 225 studies, students in lecture-only courses were 1.5 times as likely to fail as students in courses where they worked actively (Freeman et al., 2014).${cite(1)} So the teaching sits in the lessons, and the room is for doing.</li>` +
          `<li><b>You read code before you write it.</b> Each step asks you to predict, change, write or fix, adapted from PRIMM, a method built for programming beginners (Sentance, Waite &amp; Kallia, 2019).${cite(2)}</li>` +
          "<li><b>You see the result first.</b> Every lesson starts by running the finished piece, so you know what you're building before the steps open it up.</li>" +
          '<li><b>One idea per Saturday; W1 to W4 run in your browser, nothing to install.</b> Our pilot in February 2026 taught us this: six topics in one day was too fast, and installing Python cost time.</li>' +
          '</ul>',
      },
      {
        q: 'What if I get stuck?',
        lines: [
          'Every step has two hints, then the solution, and errors come with a plain explanation. An AI tutor gives hints, not answers.',
          'Look up, and the facilitator comes over.',
          'Still stuck after 10 minutes on one screen? You can move on.',
        ],
      },
      {
        q: "What if I'm faster or slower than the others?",
        points: [
          'Inside a lesson you go at your own pace.',
          'Fast, or already code? "Go further" tasks and the open brief at the end go as far as you like.',
          'The next lesson opens for everyone together.',
        ],
      },
      { q: "Is it for artists who don't want to code?", lines: ["You learn to write the code; that's the craft here. Your own photos and ideas drive the piece."] },
      { q: 'Are these the lessons on pixels2genai.art?', html: '<p>Some cover the same ideas, rebuilt for the series as short screens with code on every one. <a href="/try">Try a free sample lesson</a>.</p>' },
      {
        // FQ-A (18 Nov): replaces "Can't I learn this from videos?". Claims and refs 3–6 from
        // workshop-planning/research/in-person-vs-home-2026-11-18 (lines L1–L3, L5); the curriculum-order line was dropped
        // because the calendar arc and the cards already show Foundations, then Machine learning, then Generative AI.
        q: 'Why come to the studio if I could do this at home?',
        html:
          '<ul class="fmt-points">' +
          '<li>You could, and after the workshop you can: the lessons and the AI tutor stay open for 30 days.</li>' +
          `<li>Most people who learn alone online stop: in Harvard's and MIT's open courses, about 3 in 100 finished.${cite(3)}</li>` +
          `<li>What helps is guidance and other people. In a review of 45 studies, online learning only did better when it was guided or done together.${cite(4)}</li>` +
          `<li>Beginners get stuck right after an error, and many don't ask.${cite(5)}<sup>,</sup>${cite(6)} Here you look up and the facilitator comes over, so you leave with a finished piece.</li>` +
          '</ul>',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'On the day',
    fill: 'fill-c3',
    items: [
      {
        q: 'What do I need to bring?',
        points: [
          'Your laptop and its charger.',
          'One photo of your own. The photo stays on your laptop unless you choose to upload it.',
          'Socks or slippers: the studio corridor is shoe-free.',
          'W5 and W6 run a real image model: you need a free Google account (for Google Colab, run by Google in the US) or a laptop with an NVIDIA graphics card. On a Mac, use Colab.',
          "Without either you can still follow every step, and the model's results are made for you.",
        ],
      },
      { q: 'Is there food?', lines: ["There's no lunch break. Snacks and water are there all day; bring your own food in a sealed box."] },
      { q: 'Will I be photographed?', lines: ['We may photograph prints, screens and hands. Your face only if you say yes.'] },
      {
        q: 'Can I keep working after the day?',
        lines: [
          'Yes, for 30 days. With your code, the lessons and the AI tutor stay open until 30 days after the workshop.',
          "With the series pass, each workshop's lessons and AI tutor open on its day and stay open until 30 days after it, even if you missed that day.",
        ],
      },
      { q: 'Do I get a certificate?', lines: ["There's no official certificate. You get a beautifully designed certificate of participation."] },
    ],
  },
];

// "What comes after W6?" (CR-4): the rows that were the "Where you can go next" section. `from`/`to` are
// workshop numbers; null = the free lessons (a yellow "Free" tag).
const NEXT = [
  { title: 'Generative art and print', from: 1, to: 3, text: 'Your own series for prints, pen plotters or riso.' },
  { title: 'Data and visualisation', from: 3, to: 3, text: 'Cluster and map your own image or data collections.' },
  { title: 'Machine learning', from: 4, to: 4, text: 'A first full course in neural networks, with the basics already in hand.' },
  { title: 'Your own AI tools', from: 5, to: 6, text: 'Train and steer models on your own work.' },
  { title: 'The rest of Pixels2GenAI', from: null, to: null, text: 'The free lessons on this site, in an order you now understand.' },
];
const tagsFor = (from: number | null, to: number | null) =>
  from === null ? [] : SERIES.filter((w) => w.n >= from && w.n <= (to ?? from));
const rangeLabel = (from: number | null, to: number | null) =>
  from === null ? 'Free' : from === to ? `W${from}` : `W${from}–W${to}`;

let n = 0;
const numbered = GROUPS.map((g) => ({ ...g, items: g.items.map((it) => ({ ...it, num: String(++n).padStart(2, '0') })) }));
---

<section class="ws fmt-band faq-band" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
  <div class="series-head">
    <h2 class="ws-display series-title" id="faq-title">Questions</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="fmt-faq">
    {numbered.map((g) => (
      <div class={`fmt-faq-group ${g.fill}`}>
        <h3 class="ws-chip fmt-faq-chip">{g.name}</h3>
        {g.items.map((it) => (
          <details id={it.id} open={it.open}>
            <summary><span class="fmt-q-num" aria-hidden="true">{it.num}</span><span class="fmt-q">{it.q}</span></summary>
            <div class="fmt-a">
              {it.lines?.map((l) => <p>{l}</p>)}
              {it.points && <ul class="fmt-points">{it.points.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}</ul>}
              {it.html && <Fragment set:html={it.html} />}
              {it.next && (
                <>
                  <ul class="fmt-next">
                    {NEXT.map((r) => (
                      <li>
                        <b class="fmt-next-title">{r.title}</b>
                        <span class="fmt-next-tags">
                          <span class="sr-only">{rangeLabel(r.from, r.to)}</span>
                          {r.from === null ? (
                            <span class="fmt-tag fill-yellow" aria-hidden="true">Free</span>
                          ) : (
                            tagsFor(r.from, r.to).map((w) => <span class="fmt-tag" data-band={bandKey(w.band)} aria-hidden="true">W{w.n}</span>)
                          )}
                        </span>
                        <span class="fmt-next-text">{r.text}</span>
                      </li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                  <p class="fmt-note">These are directions, not promises.</p>
                </>
              )}
            </div>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="fmt-note fmt-faq-more">Not answered here? Write to <a href="mailto:workshops@pixels2genai.art">workshops@pixels2genai.art</a>.</p>

  <div class="fmt-refs">
    <p class="ws-label">References</p>
    <ol>
      <li id="fmt-ref-1">Freeman, S., Eddy, S. L., McDonough, M., Smith, M. K., Okoroafor, N., Jordt, H., &amp; Wenderoth, M. P. (2014). Active learning increases student performance in science, engineering, and mathematics. <i>PNAS, 111</i>(23), 8410–8415. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1319030111">https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1319030111</a></li>
      <li id="fmt-ref-2">Sentance, S., Waite, J., &amp; Kallia, M. (2019). Teaching computer programming with PRIMM: A sociocultural perspective. <i>Computer Science Education, 29</i>(2–3), 136–176. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/08993408.2019.1608781">https://doi.org/10.1080/08993408.2019.1608781</a></li>
      <li id="fmt-ref-3">Reich, J., &amp; Ruipérez-Valiente, J. A. (2019). The MOOC pivot. <i>Science, 363</i>(6423), 130–131. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aav7958">https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aav7958</a></li>
      <li id="fmt-ref-4">Means, B., Toyama, Y., Murphy, R., Bakia, M., &amp; Jones, K. (2010). <i>Evaluation of evidence-based practices in online learning: A meta-analysis and review of online learning studies</i>. U.S. Department of Education. <a href="https://internet.psych.wisc.edu/wp-content/uploads/532-Master/532-UnitPages/Unit-03/DOE_OnlineLearningMetaAnalysis_2010.pdf">https://internet.psych.wisc.edu/wp-content/uploads/532-Master/532-UnitPages/Unit-03/DOE_OnlineLearningMetaAnalysis_2010.pdf</a></li>
      <li id="fmt-ref-5">Bosch, N., &amp; D'Mello, S. (2017). The affective experience of novice computer programmers. <i>International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education, 27</i>(1), 181–206. <a href="https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1126594">https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1126594</a></li>
      <li id="fmt-ref-6">Marwan, S., Dombe, A., &amp; Price, T. W. (2020). Unproductive help-seeking in programming: What it is and how to address it. In <i>Proceedings of the 2020 ACM Conference on Innovation and Technology in Computer Science Education</i> (pp. 54–60). ACM. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3341525.3387394">https://doi.org/10.1145/3341525.3387394</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>
